finding: 1. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:149–156,341–346 — The two inventory reads short-circuit and discard partial results. If volumes list returns two volumes but machine list exits nonzero, the only report is the Machine command error; neither known billable volume is named. If volumes list fails, Machines are not queried at all. A returned list error blocks success but does not deliver the promised resource report. The spawnSync at :101–112 has no timeout, so a hung list can also prevent any report or lock release indefinitely. 2. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:149–156,322–347; src/cli/publish-lock.ts:65–70 — The post-deploy reads do not bound the accepted race at publish return. With two already-admitted deploys, A can finish its volume list with one volume; B can then create a second volume before A finishes Machine list, with B not yet creating a Machine. A passes even though two volumes already exist, and if B's parent was killed no B report ever follows. A stale-lock retry in the same checkout can overlap an orphaned flyctl this way: ESRCH automatically clears the lock without the child check described in the manual refusal guidance. The report establishes counts at separate observation times, not an inventory guarantee at return.
